  "body": "**Stacked on #1002** (issue #926, `issue/926-post-script-suites`). Until #1002 merges, this diff also shows its commit (`b471395e`). This PR adds one commit on top. Retarget/merge order per AGENTS.md \"Overlapping issues stack\": #1002 first.\n\nFork synced at v0.17.0 (PROCESS_VERSION 16). Effort: high (P0, under the safety floor: posted G-code).\n\n## What was wrong\n\nThe six posts that write canned cycles swallowed every rapid after a cycle until the section ended. They also wrote `G98` once, read while the tool already stood on R (the stream's rapid down to R is posted before the cycle), so the \"initial level\" was R. Result: the traverse between holes and the exit from the last hole ran at the R plane, lower than the hop/clearance the path was simulated and verified at.\n\n## What changed (per dialect)\n\nAll six now follow the same law (`assets/posts/AGENTS.md`, \"The hop law\"):\n\n- **The chain a cycle replaces is only what stays on its hole**: its XY, at or below R. The **first rapid that leaves the hole** (the hop up, the traverse, the exit) ends the group with `G80` and posts as the stream says. So the posted traverse is the stream's own rapids, at the stream's hop/clearance.\n- **Each hole returns to R.**\n  - `fanuc`, `haas`, `mazak`, `doosan-dnm`, `tormach` (PathPilot/LinuxCNC): a `G99` block when each cycle group opens (RS-274 / ISO 6983: G99 = return to R, G98 = return to the level the tool stood at). The once-per-program `G98` is gone.\n  - `sinumerik`: still no G98/G99 word (none invented, return mode not assumed). The tool is seated on R before the cycle is read, so either return mode lands on R.\n- **Seat (`cycleSeat`)**: a cycle read away from its hole position (a stream whose cycle is anchored on the rapid down to R) is seated first. It goes up to R when below it, then over the hole, then down, so it never runs under the stream's straight rapid. A cycle that is its section's first motion is not seated: there is no stream position yet.\n- **A cut (linear/arc) that leaves the hole inside a cycle group is refused by name** (\"a cutting move leaves its hole inside a canned-cycle group\"). Before, it was silently swallowed. Heidenhain already refused this.\n- Heidenhain and Carvera are unchanged. They already post the safe links explicitly.\n\n## Before / after (`drill_retract`: R 1, hop 3, clearance 10; fanuc)\n\nBefore (from the issue):\n```\nG00 X10 Y10 Z1\nG98\nG73 X10 Y10 Z-20 R1 Q4 F60\nG80\nG73 X30 Y10 Z-15 R1 Q4 F60   <- X10 -> X30 at Z1\nG80\nM5                            <- leaves at Z1\n```\nAfter (`crates/post/tests/recorded/golden/fanuc/drill_retract.nc`):\n```\nG00 X10 Y10 Z1\nG99\nG73 X10 Y10 Z-20 R1 Q4 F60\nG80\nG00 X10 Y10 Z3\nG00 X30 Y10 Z3\nG00 X30 Y10 Z1\nG99\nG73 X30 Y10 Z-15 R1 Q4 F60\nG80\nG00 X30 Y10 Z3\nG00 X30 Y10 Z10\nM5\n```\nApp golden `crates/app/tests/golden/nc/drill_plate.nc` (R 0 = stock top, hop 5): it used to rapid X10 to X30 at Z0. It now posts `G00 X10 Y10 Z5 / G00 X30 Y10 Z5 / G00 X30 Y10 Z0` between the holes and `G00 X30 Y10 Z5` after the last.\n\n## Goldens (all read)\n\nNew, #1002's `drill_retract` (removed from `KNOWN_BUGS`, which keeps the two non-#994 entries): `crates/post/tests/recorded/golden/{fanuc,haas,mazak,sinumerik,tormach,doosan-dnm}/drill_retract.nc`\n\nMoved, app NC goldens (`crates/app/tests/golden/nc/`):\n- `drill_plate.nc`, `drill_peck.nc`, `tap_plate.nc` (the fanuc legacy matrix)\n- `{haas,mazak,sinumerik,tormach,doosan-dnm}/{drill_plate,drill_peck,tap_plate}.nc`\n- `synth_{fanuc,haas,mazak,sinumerik,tormach,doosan-dnm}.nc`: the synthesized conformance stream anchors each cycle on its rapid to R, so the seat now posts the stream's own positioning. After the contour it first climbs to R, then goes over the hole. The stream itself traverses at R between these holes, and the posts now post exactly that.\n- `inverted/{fanuc_x,haas_x}.nc`: the inverted-axis fixture now anchors its hole on the first cut, as the generator does, so the rapid to R posts as motion before `G99`.\n\n`drill_peck` / `tap_plate` single-hole programs gain the exit rapid (`G00 ... Z6` / `Z5`), so they no longer leave the hole at R.\n\n## Tests\n\n- **New `crates/post/tests/cycle_hop.rs`**: one generic read-back checker, run on all six cycle posts. It parses the posted program as a control runs it: XY positioning at the current level, G98/G99 return, the lower level when no mode is stated, and a dogleg rapid's lower Z. It asserts that every XY traverse after the first hole is at Z >= the stream's `RetractPlane` and that the tool leaves the cut at Z >= `ClearancePlane`.\n  - `every_cycle_post_traverses_between_holes_at_the_hop`: the recorded `drill_retract` stream.\n  - `a_cycle_anchored_on_the_rapid_to_r_is_seated_first`: the same stream with the cycles anchored one move earlier (the seat path).\n  - `the_checker_catches_the_r_plane_traverse`: the checker flags the issue's program (two faults) and an unstated-mode R traverse, and passes the fixed shape.\n  - On the pre-fix posts, both stream tests fail (checked by hand by stashing the `.cps` changes).\n- `script_*::drill_retract` for the six posts (goldens above).\n- Test edits:\n  - `posting.rs::two_ops_post_to_one_program_cycles_stay_in_their_section` now asserts `G99`.\n  - `doosan_dnm_post.rs::doosan_dnm_high_speed_smoothing_aicc`: its stream had a cycle with no chain followed by a feed away. That feed was swallowed silently before and is now refused, so the stream gains the hole's chain and a leaving rapid.\n  - `inverted_support/mod.rs`: the hole is anchored on its first cut.\n\n## Gates\n\n- `cargo xtask gate quick --base upstream/main` on the final head: green on head `321df394`: post suites ok (2 s), app suites ok (1194 s, the whole app set because `inverted_support/mod.rs` is shared test input), 0 failed\n- By hand (the quick gate may not map `.cps` changes): `cargo test -p camcraft-post` (all suites, incl. every `script_*`, `recorded_streams`, `cycle_hop`) green. The app suites that post or drill (conformance, posting, golden_cl, fanuc_post, tormach_post, tormach_machines, doosan_dnm_post, program_comments, the probe_* suites, the drill*/drilling* suites, inverted_*, lathe_post*, heidenhain_*, carvera_post and the rest of the grep for drill/G73/G81/G83/.cps, 90+ binaries) are green.\n- `cargo clippy -p camcraft-post --tests -- -D warnings`: clean\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- No real control. The return semantics are from the public RS-274 / ISO 6983 cycle vocabulary. Sinumerik relies on no return mode at all, because the tool is seated on R.\n- The lathe posts (`fanuc-lathe`, `tormach-lathe`) have a similar exit-at-R shape for face drilling (single on-axis hole, no traverse). Filed as #1011, not changed here.\n- Bare X Y modal continuations now fire only for a second cycle on the same hole. A cycle read away from its hole is seated, which posts the stream's own traverse, then opens a new group.\n\nDecisions: `docs/decisions/994-cycle-hop-level.md`.\n\nChangelog: Canned-cycle posts (Fanuc, Haas, Mazak, Tormach, Doosan DNM, Sinumerik) traverse between holes and leave the last hole at the toolpath's hop/clearance height instead of the R plane (G99 per group, G80 before the stream's own hop rapids); a cut leaving a hole inside a cycle group is refused by name\n\nFixes #994\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
